izes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/jpeg" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2000x1125+0+0/resize/1100/quality/50/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F83%2F73%2F3a307a1b4a229daf6ff87a9f126f%2Fcopy-of-6-book-covers-5.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2000x1125+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F83%2F73%2F3a307a1b4a229daf6ff87a9f126f%2Fcopy-of-6-book-covers-5.jpg" class="img" alt="Hannah Lillith Assadi, Paradiso 17; Edwidge Danticat, Dèy; Julián Delgado Lopera, Pretend You&amp;#039;re Dead and I Carry You; Valeria Luiselli, Beginning Middle End; Morgan Thomas, Mad Eden; Colin Asher, The Midnight Special: The Secret Prison History of American Music; Abdelrahman ElGendy, Huna: A Memoir of Revolution, Prison, and Becoming; Camonghne Felix, Let the Poets Govern: A Declaration of Freedom; Sara Nović, Mother Tongue; Joseph Osmundson, Spawning Season: An Experiment in Queer Parenthood" loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;ol class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;The 2026 National Book Awards &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/06/nx-s1-5991463/2026-national-book-award-shortlist-finalists" target="_blank"&gt;finalists are here&lt;/a&gt;, showcasing a diverse range of literary works, from nonfiction on the prison system to a novel about a patient navigator at a gender-affirming care center.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;em&gt;Avengers: Endgame &lt;/em&gt;has &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/10/06/nx-s1-5991466/avengers-endgame-highest-box-office-ever" target="_blank"&gt;reclaimed the number one position&lt;/a&gt; on Hollywood's all-time worldwide box office chart following its recent re-release in cinemas. It first became the highest-grossing film in history in 2019.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Karl Deisseroth, Peter Hegemann and Georg Nagel have earned the 2026 Nobel Prize for Physiology or Medicine for their contributions to optogenetics, a field of neuroscience that examines the links between neuron activity and specific behaviors. Soon after learning of his win, Deisseroth &lt;a href="https://www.wbur.org/hereandnow/2026/10/05/karl-deisseroth-nobel-prize" target="_blank"&gt;spoke with &lt;em&gt;Here &amp; Now&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. (via &lt;a href="https://www.wbur.org/" target="_blank"&gt;WBUR&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This newsletter was edited by &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/people/859721331/suzanne-nuyen"target="_blank"   &gt;&lt;em&gt;Suzanne Nuyen&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/div&gt;</description>
